---
import GuideLayout from '../../components/GuideLayout.astro';

const title = 'How to Set Up 2FA on AWS IAM (MFA Authenticator)';

const description =
  'Add MFA to AWS IAM users and root accounts with a standard TOTP authenticator. Get the virtual-MFA secret key from the IAM console and generate codes locally.';

---

<GuideLayout
  serviceName="AWS"
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/guides/aws/"
  dateModified="2026-11-07"
  lastReviewed="2026-11-07"
  intro="AWS calls authenticator apps “virtual MFA devices” — it’s the same RFC 6238 TOTP underneath. A browser-based generator is a favourite for CI boxes, consoles-on-VMs, and situations where your phone isn’t the right tool. Root and IAM users each carry their own enrollment."
  minutes={4}
  params={['6 digits', '30s period', 'SHA-1']}
  ctaTitle="An MFA-less root account is your account-billing ceiling"
  ctaBody="AWS root can close the account and every dollar in it. Security-hub style baselines all start with root MFA plus IAM least-privilege — this guide gets both machines in place; keep each enrollment's secret in a separate vault slot."
  steps={[
    {
      title: 'Open the IAM console',
      body: 'Sign in as an admin at console.aws.amazon.com → IAM → Users → pick the user → “Security credentials” tab. For the root user: IAM console home shows an “Add MFA” card for root — the enrollment lives there, not in a user profile.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Assign an MFA device',
      body: 'Click “Assign MFA device”, choose “Authenticator app” (the virtual-MFA option), and continue. AWS picks an arbitrary account name for display — it doesn’t affect anything.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Get the secret key',
      body: 'AWS shows a QR code. Click “Show secret key” below it and copy the Base32 string — that’s the secret you’d normally only scan.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Generate two consecutive codes',
      body: 'Paste the key into 2facounter.com. AWS asks for **two consecutive codes** — copy the current token, wait for the next 30-second step (watch the ring), then paste the fresh one.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Confirm activation',
      body: 'Enter both codes and click “Add MFA”. The device flips to “Active”. Test sign-in once immediately — if a code is rejected at sign-in, it’s usually clock drift, so check your device time sync.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Back up the key',
      body: 'The key is re-derivable only if you saved it: keep it encrypted in your password manager, because IAM won’t show it again after activation (deactivate and re-assign to rotate).',
    },
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'What about the root account?',
      a: 'Root MFA is configured from the IAM console’s root card or Account settings page. Recent AWS guidance strongly prefers passkeys for root — set one if you can, but a TOTP device remains supported and works identically with this generator.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Why does AWS require two codes in a row?',
      a: 'To prove you truly control a synced authenticator rather than holding one leaked screenshot. Two codes from different time windows show the secret is live and your clock matches the server’s.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Can I enforce MFA on every IAM user with policy?',
      a: 'Yes — an IAM policy condition on aws:MultiFactorAuthPresent turns MFA from advice into policy for console sessions, and AWS’s Well-Architected guidance treats that baseline as a required control. Enrollment per user (this guide) is the prerequisite.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Can I use one MFA entry for multiple AWS accounts?',
      a: 'Each IAM user or root enrollment mints its own secret — never reuse keys across accounts. Generate a separate recent-key slot here for each enrollment; duplicate keys are an audit finding and a blast-radius problem.',
    },
  ]}
/>
